{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/NodeContents]/fn[fingerprint]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "These checks load and store recorded dependencies without checking their\n freshness. Fingerprinting is outside the storage and output-decoding\n operations being tested, so this guard must remain unused.\n Other domain fixtures exercise tracked fingerprint observations."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs` `impl[StoredGraph/NodeContents]/fn[fingerprint]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Form:** It is fenced citation commentary on an explicit function with a body, which is what the built-in asks for. It explains why the function has zero execution, and it gives a reason beyond difficulty.\n- **Substance:** `fingerprint` is a guard that panics, and `NodeContents` requires the method. The storage tests only load and store records, or decode outputs, so they should never resolve contents. A call would mean an unwanted graph access.\n- **Agreement with the source:**\n  - The storage tests in `storage.rs` call only `FileEvidenceStorage` and the `StoredGraph` `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` delegations, and none of them calls `fingerprint`.\n  - The `Validator` test `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors` runs `StoredReview`, whose `execute` records no tracked observations. That test therefore has no content or edge dependencies to check, so the guard stays unused, as the commentary says.\n  - The claim that other fixtures exercise fingerprints is also true. `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs`, `contracts.rs` and `validation.rs` each give `fingerprint` a real implementation.\n- **Citation role:** `{cite}` is the permitted form for this built-in. The item carries no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target, so the citation is not redundant.\n\nI found no gap.",
